# Movement Work
Tamil title: கழகப் பணி

Kovilpatti

Kadambur

Ettayapuram

Kolathur

Eral

Nazareth

Panagudi

Thisayanvilai

Melapalayam

Mukkudal

At the arrangement of comrade Vallimuthu, secretary of the DMK in Tirunelveli district, I toured the towns listed above from the fifteenth to the twentieth of this month. I gained an opportunity to learn how thoroughly Tirunelveli district had been prepared. Everywhere was filled with awakening.

There were two meetings each day. On the road from one town to the next, many small villages offered welcomes and words of goodwill. Every meeting was large enough for the public to say that their town had never before seen one of that scale.

When one thinks of Tirunelveli district, ordinarily only the names Tuticorin and Kovilpatti come to mind. The enthusiasm I saw in the district's interior during these five days was truly astonishing. Courageous young people are working in all these towns. Village elders too welcome us, saying our movement is the great medicine that will cure the people's decline.

In many places, movement offices and reading rooms have been properly established, and public meetings were organised skilfully. In large towns with extensive movement connections, we often see crowds disperse when the conch sounds or diminish when train time arrives. Nothing of that sort appeared at the public meetings in Tirunelveli's interior towns.

People from surrounding villages and local comrades treated each meeting as a valuable opportunity that had to be used completely. Just as religious devotees gather with their families for a festival, they came in their thousands, though not without thought, and kept the meetings full of energy even after midnight.

When I say midnight, it is not an ornament of speech. I mean it literally.

When we reached the Mukkudal meeting, it was nearly twelve at night. The meeting continued until two.

At Thisayanvilai, after finishing the meeting, we sat down to eat the loving six-flavoured meal served by movement comrades. It was two in the morning.

Every town conducted its public meeting like a conference of its own. Throughout the town were decorative arches, welcome arches, placards bearing messages, drums and music, flags, processions and the sound of fireworks.

In the procession organised by comrades at Eral, some carried ploughs along with the flag. The sight demonstrated that the hearts of farmers are with us.

In many towns, those now working for our movement were formerly in Congress. Nor had they merely stood somewhere in a corner for form's sake; they had been in the front rank. They accepted hardship and loss for Congress's growth. Then they saw their labour being used for the lives of parasites.

Congress had risen to become the party governing their towns, but neglected genuine workers and local welfare while waving ceremonial fans over hypocrites and black-market vultures. Unable to bear that degradation, these comrades left it. A flame of determination arose within them to establish good government for the country, whatever hardship and loss it required. They joined the people's party, the DMK, and now work with distinction. I saw many such comrades in Ettayapuram, birthplace of the national poet Bharati.

Bharati sang:

“We shall bow to agriculture and labour;
we shall condemn those who eat and grow fat in idleness.”

Under the rule of those who hunted votes using Bharati's name, conditions have grown so terrible that people wonder whether even tamarind seeds and oilcake will remain to eat. Could Ettayapuram see this and not throb with anguish? That is why its comrades welcome the Dravidian movement.

Tirunelveli is a region filled with eminent Saivites. Until ten years ago, many there also thought us empty of knowledge in religious matters. Today they appreciate the views we present about religion. They rejoice upon recognising that the Dravidian movement performs the noble task of cleansing stains from people's minds.

While I stayed at Tuticorin and Tirunelveli, many communist and socialist comrades spoke with me. Every conversation concerned the next election.

Comrade Ponnambalam, who works for the socialist movement in Tirunelveli district, met us at the lodging when we returned to Tirunelveli at three in the morning after the Mukkudal meeting. He spoke with me for a long time about the Socialist Party's position and the Dravidian movement's course.

In general, members of all parties are thinking intensely about the next election. They strongly believe that the cooperation and support of the rapidly growing DMK would benefit their respective parties. At public meetings and in private conversations, I clearly explained our organisation's position regarding the election. Many were disappointed.

Everyone who genuinely lives and works among the public fears that if the country is trapped under Congress rule once again, a destructive disease like Nazism will seize it. Ordinary people say no party has ever acquired as much strength and influence as Congress, but no party has declined so rapidly either. Rural areas are filled with bitterness to that extent.

During these four years of Congress rule, the petty jungle kings, black-marketeers, hypocrites and capitalists of every locality have put on khadi shirts. This sight alone is enough to open people's eyes. Whoever commits injustice and grows prosperous by influence is “there,” meaning inside Congress. That is why the public has developed a powerful conviction that Congress must be defeated.

If a beautifully fashioned pillar in one's own house becomes eaten by termites, does one remain idle thinking about its beauty, workmanship and the labour once spent installing it? Knowing that a termite-eaten pillar threatens the whole building, one removes it. So it is with Congress.

Thoughtful people ask what horror this is. A few young men, countable on one's fingers, worked to transform the Justice Party, once the dwelling place of imperial servants, into a people's movement. Yet Congress, nourished by Kumaran's blood, has today been turned into the ceremonial hall of cruel men who drink the poor person's blood.

Having seen the condition of Tirunelveli district, I promised to send our movement's comrades into many more small villages there to carry Dravidian movement propaganda.

We greatly need comrades able to serve full time. The work is abundant, and conditions for new camps arise continually. The number of workers must increase. The movement's machinery has grown large, but the present staff is insufficient.

*Dravida Nadu*, 6 May 1951
